        {
          "text": "The primary objective of poisoning attacks on sequential recommenders is to promote a target item's visibility with minimal injected data.",
          "cite_keys": [],
          "evidence_ids": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Attackers achieve this by injecting a small number of synthetic user profiles whose interaction sequences are crafted to bias the model's learned preferences.",
          "cite_keys": [],
          "evidence_ids": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Empirical results show that a 0.5% injection budget can raise the target item's hit rate from 0.7% to 14.2% on MovieLens-1M.",
          "cite_keys": ["lovelace2025poison"],
          "evidence_ids": ["e-1"]
        },
        {
          "text": "This twenty-fold increase demonstrates that even a tiny fraction of malicious profiles can dramatically alter ranking outcomes.",
          "cite_keys": ["lovelace2025poison"],
          "evidence_ids": ["e-1"]
        },
        {
          "text": "The cost to the attacker is therefore very low relative to the potential impact, making such attacks practical in real-world systems.",
          "cite_keys": [],
          "evidence_ids": []
        },
        {
          "text": "However, the reported result is limited to a single dataset and target item, so the generalizability of this injection rate remains uncertain.",
