        {
          "text": "Sequential recommender systems model user behavior as ordered sequences of interactions, such as clicks or purchases, to predict the next item a user will engage with.",
          "cite_keys": [],
          "evidence_ids": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Unlike traditional collaborative filtering, which treats user-item interactions as independent, sequential recommenders explicitly capture temporal dynamics and short-term intent through architectures such as recurrent neural networks, self-attention, and convolutional sequence models.",
          "cite_keys": [],
          "evidence_ids": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The growing reliance on sequential models in e-commerce, streaming, and social media has raised concerns about their robustness to adversarial manipulation of training data, commonly referred to as poisoning attacks.",
          "cite_keys": [],
          "evidence_ids": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Poisoning attacks inject carefully crafted fake user profiles or interaction sequences into the training set with the goal of altering the model's recommendations for specific target items or degrading overall performance.",
          "cite_keys": [],
          "evidence_ids": []
        },
        {
          "text": "This review examines the research question of how poisoning attacks affect sequential recommenders, focusing on the mechanisms by which malicious sequences influence model behavior and the extent to which existing detection methods can mitigate such threats.",
          "cite_keys": [],
          "evidence_ids": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The scope includes both white-box attacks, where the attacker has full knowledge of the model and training data, and black-box attacks, where only limited information is available, as well as targeted and untargeted attack objectives.",
          "cite_keys": [],
          "evidence_ids": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Because no located fulltext evidence is available for this review, the findings presented here are limited to a conceptual synthesis of the attack surface and defense landscape, without quantitative results or empirical comparisons.",
          "cite_keys": [],
          "evidence_ids": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The review is organized around three main sections: attack objectives, which describe what attackers aim to achieve; detection limits, which discuss the challenges and current approaches for identifying poisoned data; and a concluding synthesis of open problems.",
          "cite_keys": [],
          "evidence_ids": []
        }
